{
  "scene": {
    "id": "distribute",
    "language": "(inhales and exhales deeply) Okay, so in this scenario, we see that our left winger is not marked. Our right winger is marked. Our left striker is in a poor position between two defenders, and our right striker is open. So we have our left winger open and our right striker open. If... (sighs) So you have two passing options. You could either pass to your right striker and make a run, or you can pass to your right striker and your left striker w- and the- or your right striker would pass to your left striker, and your left striker will take the shot. But I think in this scenario,[0] the better play would be to pass to the left winger, although it sounds counterintuitive. So you pass.[1] Yeah, so after you pass,[2] now your left winger, like look at the direction he's facing. He can pass to your left striker, and your left striker is in a better position to finish. After you pass, you can still... You shouldn't make the run as a midfielder. You should stay here in case that defender starts pressing, and this defender starts pressing, and then you should back up and give your left winger space. Give your left winger an option to pass back. But once your left winger receives the ball, he should immediately pass to your left striker. Your left striker should turn and shoot. If not, your left striker can also, like pass to your right striker, and like the game, uh, and the, and the flow of the game will dictate what decision will be made best. Like after the left winger passes, he can also make a run. But yeah, I think that's more than enough detail for just the midfielder part.",
